From 8070d75216faed8e75af23be3382751ffad63ac4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hermes Local <hermes-local@r730-001>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 00:23:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(config): one knobs bounds every human-decision wait
 (answer/confirm/permission)

approvals.timeout already bounded the dangerous-command prompt, but every OTHER surface
parked on the operator hardcoded its own short wait, so configuring a long window did not
actually mean a long window:

  slash-confirm pending (gateway)   300s  -> approvals.timeout (live)
  model-switch warning modal        120s  -> approvals.timeout (live)
  _prompt_text_input_modal default  120s  -> approvals.timeout (live)
  sudo password prompt               45s  -> approvals.timeout (live)
  vault unlock / save-login / code  120/180s -> approvals.timeout (live)
  secret_capture callback           120s  -> approvals.timeout (live)
  qqbot approval card lifetime      300s  -> approvals.timeout (live)

Added tools.approval_context.human_confirm_timeout() as the single resolver (same knob,
same MAX_SAFE_TIMEOUT_S clamp, <=0 = unlimited) and tools.slash_confirm.resolve_timeout()
for the gateway primitive; resolve()/clear_if_stale() now default to it so a button tapped
hours later still lands instead of reading as stale.

tests/tools/test_human_decision_timeout.py guards it: resolver tracks the knob, honours
unlimited, stays platform-safe-capped, a 1h-old pending confirm survives a 24h window and
is correctly stale under 300s, and a late button tap runs the handler.
